public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = arr.length - 1;

    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = (left + right) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
            left = mid + 1;
        } else {
            right = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    return -1; // Element not found
}
```

The `binarySearch` method takes two parameters: an integer array `arr` and an integer `target`. It initializes two variables `left` and `right` as the first and last indices of the array respectively.

It then enters a loop that continues until `left` becomes greater than or equal to `right`.

Inside the loop, it calculates the middle index `mid` using integer division (`/`) by adding `left` and `right` together.

If the value at the middle index `arr[mid]` is equal to the target, we have found our element and can return its index. If it's less than the target, we update `left` to be `mid + 1`, which means we now only need to search the right half of the array. If it's greater than the target, we update `right` to be `mid - 1`, which means we now only need to search the left half of the array.

If the loop completes without finding the target, we return `-1` to indicate that the element was not found in the array.

